{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>He gave you treasure in your sacks.</b> Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 43:23): It is possible that a man has treasure (מַטְמוֹן) in his house and forgets it, and it went out with your lot — 'for your money came to me.' Now these are words of comfort, for how could each one's money go out by its exact weight? Rather, anything hidden is called מַטְמוֹן, as in 'The slothful man hid (טָמַן) his hand in the dish' (Proverbs 19:24), 'We have treasures (מַטְמֹנִים) in the field' (Jeremiah 41:8). And so he told them that it is the way of the donkey-drivers who purchase grain for each one to place his money in his sack, and the provider (הַמַּשְׁבִּיר) took the sacks of those who came and filled ten sacks with grain, with the silver hidden (טָמוּן) beneath the grain, and his master came and commanded him to pour them out into the vessels of Jacob's sons — either because he wished to hurry to send them off, or because the silver had not come to him. And so each one's money was in the mouth of his sack by its exact weight, for each man would bring in exchange for his produce silver equal in value to a donkey-load of grain. And this occurs constantly in the markets and in the storehouses sold during great turmoil among the people."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 43, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:22:25.210304043Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}